00:20:16,100 --> 00:20:21,000
really fast but take a lot of hardware. Some are pretty slow, but don't take

208
00:20:21,000 --> 00:20:27,060
much hardware at all. And so they managed to create this this abstraction that's called the

209
00:20:27,060 --> 00:20:32,680
instruction set architecture, which is what the target of a compiler should be, and then let the

210
00:20:32,680 --> 00:20:38,080
hardware people figure out how best to implement it. And then the lower, so that's a concept

211
00:20:38,080 --> 00:20:46,630
actually that came along in the 1960s. So that's even before my time, as far as a computer.

212
00:20:46,650 --> 00:20:53,600
person, but it's a very important concept in the world of computers.

213
00:20:53,600 --> 00:20:59,180
And then what they call the lower level stuff, how it actually gets implemented is called the microarchitecture.

214
00:20:59,180 --> 00:21:03,660
We will talk very, very little about microarchitecture in this course.

215
00:21:03,660 --> 00:21:10,460
And as I mentioned, machine code is sort of a generic term that incorporates both the actual bits that

216
00:21:10,460 --> 00:21:19,470
are operating, executing as well as the assembly level version of it. And as I mentioned, you can

217
00:21:19,470 --> 00:21:26,750
think of from an instruction set point of view. So IA32, x86, 64, and what's called

218
00:21:26,750 --> 00:21:36,390
itanium, or this not very successful Intel thing, those are all in some ways different instruction set

219
00:21:36,390 --> 00:21:42,030
architectures. Arm actually, similar to Intel, has gone through various different generations.

220
00:21:42,030 --> 00:21:49,620
So there's various different generations of its instruction set. So from a programmer, machine

221
00:21:49,620 --> 00:21:56,420
At the machine level programmers perspective, things are a bit different than you see when you write C code.

222
00:21:56,420 --> 00:22:03,360
First of all, there is some very visible parts of the instruction, the machine state, that you can examine

223
00:22:03,360 --> 00:22:08,960
and test and operate on, and you must, in fact, that you would never understand what those

224
00:22:08,960 --> 00:22:11,890
are if you're just thinking in terms of C.

225
00:22:11,890 --> 00:22:15,950
So in particular, there's some sort of a program counter.

226
00:22:15,950 --> 00:22:20,110
It's a, it tells you what address is the instruction that you where, what address is the

227
00:22:20,110 --> 00:22:24,110
instruction that you're going to execute next, where is that located in memory?

228
00:22:24,110 --> 00:22:33,030
And then there's a set of registers which are part of the, that the programmer actually makes use of,

229
00:22:33,030 --> 00:22:40,490
you can think of them as a very small number of memory locations, but rather than giving an address

230
00:22:40,490 --> 00:22:48,490
from zero up to n minus one or something, you actually give them by name specifically. And then

231
00:22:48,490 --> 00:22:52,850
And then there's another sort of state that's just a few bits worth of state that

232
00:22:52,850 --> 00:23:01,290
talk about what what are the results of some recent instructions where they, did it produce a value

233
00:23:01,290 --> 00:23:04,650
of zero, did it produce a negative or a positive value?

234
00:23:04,650 --> 00:23:10,210
And those are used to implement conditional branching, which we'll look at later as we go.

235
00:23:10,210 --> 00:23:14,630
And then the other part of it, so that's sort of the processor, and then the other part of

236
00:23:14,630 --> 00:23:16,310
of it is the memory.

237
00:23:16,310 --> 00:23:23,030
And as I mentioned in one of the first lectures, the memory is you can think

238
00:23:23,030 --> 00:23:26,760
of logically as just an array of bytes.

239
00:23:26,760 --> 00:23:31,440
And that's what the machine level programmer sees.

240
00:23:31,440 --> 00:23:34,700
And it's actually kind of a fiction in different ways.

241
00:23:34,700 --> 00:23:39,740
As I mentioned before, there's sort of a collaboration between the operating system and the hardware,

242
00:23:39,740 --> 00:23:46,000
what they call virtual memory, to make it look like each program running on a processor has its

243
00:23:46,000 --> 00:23:53,030
own independent array of bytes that it can access, even though they actually share

244
00:23:53,030 --> 00:24:00,970
values within the physical memory itself. And furthermore, you heard the term cache. The idea of a cache

245
00:24:00,970 --> 00:24:08,290
is not visible here at all because it just is automatically loaded with recent stuff,

246
00:24:08,290 --> 00:24:13,990
and the only thing that will look different is if you re-access that memory, it will go faster than it would

247
00:24:13,990 --> 00:24:16,270
if it hadn't been cached. But it's not

248
00:24:16,270 --> 00:24:21,390
visible in terms of there's no instructions to manipulate the cache. There's no way you

249
00:24:21,390 --> 00:24:31,300
can directly access the cache. So you already see that it's already abstract. It's got some more

250
00:24:31,300 --> 00:24:38,200
details of real hardware, but underneath it, the sort of microarchitecture level, has some features

251
00:24:38,200 --> 00:24:47,660
built into it that you're not operating on, you're not making use of directly when

252
00:24:47,740 --> 00:24:57,610
write machine level programs. So if you have a program then, say in C, it would

253
00:24:57,610 --> 00:25:04,370
typically, on a larger program, it will include multiple files, and it will make use of some library

254
00:25:04,370 --> 00:25:11,670
code. And so the process of compilation is actually a series of steps that will take what you've

255
00:25:11,670 --> 00:25:19,400
written for code, turn it into machine code, combine it with the compiled, compiler-generated

256
00:25:19,400 --> 00:25:26,080
code for the libraries and produce finally a file that's your actual executable

257
00:25:26,080 --> 00:25:32,940
program. And so that's shown here in this picture that the first step is to take C and actually generate

258
00:25:32,940 --> 00:25:39,360
