00:34:28,950 --> 00:34:34,950
So what are some characteristics then of assembly code, especially how do they differ from C?

363
00:34:34,950 --> 00:34:42,950
Well first of all there's a number of different sort of integer data types of size 1, 2, 4, and 8 bytes.

364
00:34:42,950 --> 00:34:47,930
In integer data types, they don't distinguish sign versus unsigned.

365
00:34:47,950 --> 00:34:52,140
in how it gets stored.

366
00:34:52,140 --> 00:34:57,140
And even an address or a pointer is just stored as a number in a computer

367
00:34:57,140 --> 00:35:01,140
and doesn't have any special significance to it.

368
00:35:01,140 --> 00:35:04,430
A floating point is handled in a very different way,

369
00:35:04,430 --> 00:35:07,430
on the other hand, with a different set of registers

370
00:35:07,430 --> 00:35:12,430
that I think we'll talk about very briefly in one of the later lectures.

371
00:35:12,430 --> 00:35:16,430
The program itself is in x86, it's just a series of bytes,

372
00:35:16,430 --> 00:35:18,430
and I'll show you some examples of those.

373
00:35:18,430 --> 00:35:27,430
And things like arrays and structs and things that you think of as fundamental data types don't exist at the machine level.

374
00:35:27,430 --> 00:35:31,430
They're sort of constructed artificially by the compiler.

375
00:35:31,430 --> 00:35:37,430
And later in the course, in these lectures, we'll cross over into how that's actually done as well.

376
00:35:37,430 --> 00:35:47,590
So some of the things you know about in C exist here, and some of them are sort of have to be built up in layers on top of the assembly.

377
00:35:47,590 --> 00:35:49,590
of the assembly level program.

378
00:35:49,590 --> 00:35:56,090
So the other thing about assembly level programming is each instruction is

379
00:35:56,090 --> 00:35:59,090
each instruction is very, very limited in what it can do.

380
00:35:59,090 --> 00:36:08,090
It can move data from a register to memory, from, or it can do an addition or a multiplication,

381
00:36:08,090 --> 00:36:13,410
or something like that, but it can really basically only do one thing.

382
00:36:13,410 --> 00:36:21,050
And so you have to write a whole, if you were writing it by hand, you'd have to write a whole series of

383
00:36:21,050 --> 00:36:23,050
of instructions to get anything done.

384
00:36:23,050 --> 00:36:27,050
And that's part of the reason why it's really much better to let the compiler do that.

385
00:36:27,050 --> 00:36:33,170
And we'll also see there's sort of how things like do loops, while loops,

386
00:36:33,170 --> 00:36:41,780
while loops, conditionals, switch statements, those are all built up on top of some other

387
00:36:41,780 --> 00:36:47,610
lower level features in the instructions.

388
00:36:47,610 --> 00:36:53,590
So for this function, some store, actually it gets encoded by a total of

389
00:36:53,610 --> 00:36:55,610
14 bytes.

390
00:36:55,610 --> 00:37:00,610
And so one thing about x86 is some instructions are as short as one byte,

391
00:37:00,610 --> 00:37:05,670
but others can be as long as 15 bytes in the encoding.

392
00:37:05,670 --> 00:37:17,980
And like I said, each each instruction really typically only does one thing.

393
00:37:17,980 --> 00:37:23,070
So for example, in C, if you say star-dast equals t,

394
00:37:23,070 --> 00:37:29,070
what that will typically, and not always, but the sort of way to think about that at the machine level, is it's

395
00:37:29,070 --> 00:37:34,070
T or some local value would be typically stored in a register.

396
00:37:34,070 --> 00:37:39,070
And as you know and see, if you put star in front of it, you want it referenced as a pointer.

397
00:37:39,070 --> 00:37:44,330
And if that reference is on the left, you want to store a number or value

398
00:37:44,330 --> 00:37:47,330
at that place where you're pointing to.

399
00:37:47,330 --> 00:37:53,330
So what would typically happen is desk, the actual pointer value,

400
00:37:53,330 --> 00:37:55,330
would also be stored in a register.

401
00:37:55,330 --> 00:37:58,420
Here it's in register RBX.

402
00:37:58,420 --> 00:37:59,420
And I use a move.

403
00:37:59,420 --> 00:38:10,420
instruction to say take the value from one register, which is called RAX here, and store it in the memory location that's specified by another register.

404
00:38:10,420 --> 00:38:17,420
So you see the idea, we'll go into more detail about all these register names and what the parentheses mean.

405
00:38:17,420 --> 00:38:27,670
But you get the rough idea that a move instruction is saying can refer to either a register or a memory location.

406
00:38:27,670 --> 00:38:30,650
And the actual object code representation of this is,

407
00:38:30,670 --> 00:38:38,210
is just three bytes, where the first byte tells it that it,

408
00:38:38,210 --> 00:38:43,210
and we won't even talk much in this course about how the instructions are encoded.

409
00:38:43,210 --> 00:38:47,210
Won't generally let programs do that for us.

410
00:38:47,210 --> 00:38:52,300
But in this case, it only takes three bytes to write that particular instruction.

411
00:38:52,850 --> 00:38:55,850
So how can we know that kind of stuff?

412
00:38:55,850 --> 00:38:57,850
How can we figure out that level of detail?

413
00:38:57,850 --> 00:39:01,850
Well, there's some really useful tools that let you examine machine code

414
00:39:01,850 --> 00:39:09,850
code, even if you don't have a copy of the original C file or even the assembly code file ahead of time.

415
00:39:09,850 --> 00:39:11,850
And there's one called a disassembler.

416
00:39:11,850 --> 00:39:19,850
So an assembler goes from this text version of instructions to a byte-level representation.

417
00:39:19,850 --> 00:39:22,850
And a disassembler just reverses that.

418
00:39:22,850 --> 00:39:24,850
It says, here is a series of bytes.

419
00:39:24,850 --> 00:39:32,070
And I, the disassembler, know that this particular byte sequence

420
00:39:32,070 --> 00:39:34,070
refers is a move instruction.

421
00:39:34,070 --> 00:39:39,070
And so it will now print out on the screen to say, oh, that was a move instruction.

422
00:39:39,070 --> 00:39:45,070
And so it basically reverse engineers from the object code back to the assembly code,

423
00:39:45,070 --> 00:39:48,070
or something sort of like the assembly code.

424
00:39:48,070 --> 00:39:56,220
You'll see it lost, it's very slightly different.

425
00:39:56,220 --> 00:40:02,220
One thing also to notice I should mention about assembly code is all those

426
00:40:02,220 --> 00:40:12,220
All those, you know, all the names I used, all the names of variables are completely lost at the assembly code level, or at the machine code level.

427
00:40:12,220 --> 00:40:15,220
Things are just, they're in registers, they're somewhere in memory.

